WEBVTT

00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:15.740
<v Ira Glass>Dave was in his late 20s, and it would not be accurate to say that he was living at home with his parents, but only because half the time he was staying at his sister's house. He was playing in a band, doing some writing, not making much money, and his parents were worried. And one day he was hanging out with his mom.
<v Ira Glass>戴夫快三十岁了，如果说他住在父母家并不准确，因为他有一半时间都待在他姐姐家。他当时在一个乐队里，偶尔写点东西，挣不到什么钱，他的父母很担心他。有一天，他和妈妈待在一起。

00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:23.640
<v Dave Hill>We were coming home from going ice skating together, which right there you can see that out. To be fair, I like ice skating. I'm a quarter Canadian.
<v Dave Hill>我们当时一起滑冰回家，从这一点你就能看出来。公平地说，我喜欢滑冰。我有四分之一的加拿大血统。

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:27.330
<v Ira Glass>Wait, wait, wait. You can see what? What does that show that you went ice skating with your mom?
<v Ira Glass>等等，等等。你能看出什么？你和你妈妈去滑冰说明了什么？

00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:42.630
<v Dave Hill>Well, most guys, after a certain age, wouldn't go regularly ice skating with their mom to like [? organ ?] sessions where you're skating arm in arm around. I mean this is the sort of stuff I would do with my mom, like pretty regularly.
<v Dave Hill>嗯，大多数男人到了某个年纪，就不会经常陪妈妈去那种滑冰场，在那里你们还要手挽着手滑。我的意思是，这就是我跟我妈会做的事情，而且挺频繁的。

00:00:42.630 --> 00:01:00.010
<v Ira Glass>So they pull into the driveway. Dave's mom tells him there's going to be a benefit for this retirement home for nuns and priests. His mom's an old school Catholic who goes to mass every day. And she says it's going to be at a hotel in downtown Cleveland. They live in Cleveland. And Maureen McGovern, who sang the theme to The Poseidon Adventure back in the '70s was going to be the entertainment.
<v Ira Glass>他们开车驶入车道。戴夫的妈妈告诉他，要为修女和神父的养老院举办一场慈善义演。他妈妈是个老派的天主教徒，每天都去做弥撒。她说活动将在克利夫兰市中心的一家酒店举行。他们住在克利夫兰。而且，曾在70年代演唱过《海神号遇险记》主题曲的莫琳·麦戈文将担任表演嘉宾。

00:01:00.010 --> 00:01:35.740
<v Dave Hill>And she's like, so I'm getting tickets. So let me know if you want to go. And I said, no, I don't want to go. And then, so she just said, OK, well just let me know then. She's like, because I have to get the tickets in the next couple of weeks. And I figured, I didn't give it any thought, but then it kept coming up over and over. Like, let me know, the benefit's coming up and I need to buy tickets. So are you going to go? And I said, no. And she said, all right, well I need to know by next week if you're going to go. And this just went on and on for a couple of weeks.
<v Dave Hill>她说她要买票，让我考虑一下要不要去。我说不，我不想去。然后她说，好吧，到时候通知我。她说因为她得在未来几周内把票买好。我当时没怎么多想，但这件事被反复提起。比如，她说活动快到了，她需要买票，所以你到底去不去？我说不去。她说，好吧，那下周之前我得知道你是否会去。这种情况持续了好几周。

00:01:35.740 --> 00:01:50.910
<v Dave Hill>This was a big thing. My mom kept saying like, and there will be nuns and priests there. Like they're going to get some of the old nuns and priests from the retirement place out. You can walk up and touch them and stuff. I don't know why she made that a selling point.
<v Dave Hill>这可是件大事。我妈一直说，到时候会有修女和神父在那儿。就好像他们要把养老院里的一些老修女和老神父接出来一样。你可以走过去摸摸他们之类的。我不知道她为什么要把这当作一个卖点。

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:54.070
<v Ira Glass>Maybe that was a selling point for her. Maybe she likes nuns and priests.
<v Ira Glass>也许这对她来说是个卖点。也许她喜欢修女和神父。

00:01:54.070 --> 00:01:54.610
<v Dave Hill>She does.
<v Dave Hill>她确实喜欢。

00:01:54.610 --> 00:01:57.550
<v Ira Glass>And she knows some of the old ones from back in the day.
<v Ira Glass>而且她认识一些当年在那里的老人家。

00:01:57.550 --> 00:01:59.980
<v Dave Hill>Oh yeah, she's super into nuns and priests.
<v Dave Hill>哦，是的，她超级迷恋修女和神父。

00:01:59.980 --> 00:02:09.300
<v Ira Glass>And these weren't just the regular nuns and priests that she see's everyday on the ball field. These are some of the older players. You have the cards, you know, but you never see out of the stadium anymore.
<v Ira Glass>而且这些并不是她平时在球场上见到的那种普通修女和神父。这些都是一些老面孔。就像你有球员卡一样，你知道，但在球场外你再也见不到他们了。

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:28.050
<v Dave Hill>Yeah, exactly. It was like the fantasy league. Like Father Mackey from St. Anne's in 1952.
<v Dave Hill>对，没错。就像梦幻联盟一样。就像1952年圣安妮教堂的麦基神父。

00:02:28.050 --> 00:03:05.460
<v Dave Hill>So there's a lot of that going on. And then, somewhere along the line, my mom brings up the fact that a priest that used to teach at my high school when I was there, Father Dennis, was recovering from two heart attacks. And so she's like, you know, I ran into Father Dennis up at church. And I was telling him about the benefit. And he was really into it. Yeah, I bet he would like to come to this benefit also. Why don't you bring him? And I was like, I'm not going. And she's like, well, I already told him you were going to call.
<v Dave Hill>所以很多这类事情在发生。后来，我妈提到了我高中时的一位老师丹尼斯神父，他当时心脏病发作两次，正在康复中。我妈说，我在教堂碰到了丹尼斯神父，我跟他说了那个义演的事。他非常有兴趣。是啊，我打赌他也想来参加这个义演。你为什么不带他去呢？我说我不去。她说，可是我已经告诉他你会给他打电话了。

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:11.620
<v Ira Glass>So Dave realizes he's trapped. Checkmate. He's going to have to at least call Father Dennis.
<v Ira Glass>所以戴夫意识到他被困住了。将死。他至少得给丹尼斯神父打个电话。

00:03:11.620 --> 00:03:17.220
<v Ira Glass>And Father Dennis, was he just like another teacher at the school or was he somebody who you especially liked?
<v Ira Glass>那么丹尼斯神父，他只是学校里的另一位老师，还是你特别喜欢的人？

00:03:17.220 --> 00:03:31.240
<v Dave Hill>I liked him. Yeah, I mean I knew him. He ran the choir. Yeah, I thought he was a great guy. He loaned me his guitar. He played guitar. So it wasn't like we had kept in touch or anything. You know, I had fond memories of him. But I hadn't seen him in years.
<v Dave Hill>我喜欢他。是的，我的意思是，我认识他。他负责唱诗班。我觉得他是个好人。他还把吉他借给我用。他会弹吉他。我们并没有保持联系。你知道，我有关于他的美好回忆，但我已经很多年没见过他了。

00:03:31.240 --> 00:03:36.760
<v Ira Glass>So he gets Father Dennis on the phone and they agree that Dave is going to pick him up and they'll attend the event together.
<v Ira Glass>于是他给丹尼斯神父打了电话，他们约定由戴夫去接他，然后一起参加活动。

00:03:36.760 --> 00:03:48.220
<v Ira Glass>Comes the big day, it goes exactly as you might imagine a lunchtime fundraiser for retired nuns and priests in a hotel in downtown Cleveland with Maureen McGovern singing usually goes. Now there were a couple of wild card moments to the day that I'll run through quickly for you.
<v Ira Glass>大日子到了，活动正如你所想的那样，在克利夫兰市中心的一家酒店里举行了一场为退休修女和神父举办的午餐筹款会，还有莫琳·麦戈文的歌唱表演。在那一天，还有几个突发状况，我很快给你讲讲。

00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:59.010
<v Ira Glass>First, Dave runs into a friend at the event from grade school-- now gay-- whose Dave mom is convinced that they should set up with one of Dave's sisters, even though Dave tries to keep explaining to her that Guy is gay.
<v Ira Glass>首先，戴夫在活动中遇到了一个小学同学——现在是个男同性恋——戴夫的妈妈确信应该把他介绍给戴夫的一个姐妹，尽管戴夫一直在向她解释那个人是同性恋。

00:03:59.010 --> 00:04:12.340
<v Dave Hill>And she thinks that I'm being mean, insulting him by calling him gay. I'm like, no, he's a gay man. That's fine. And just explain to me why it's not going to work out?
<v Dave Hill>她觉得我说话刻薄，通过称他为同性恋来侮辱他。我说，不，他就是个男同性恋，这没关系。你能跟我解释一下为什么这事儿行不通吗？

00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:25.070
<v Ira Glass>Second, the luncheon buffet turns out to be mostly fried foods. None of which can be eaten by Father Dennis, who's still recovering from his two heart attacks and requested a special low fat meal. Though Dave's mom has come prepared for this.
<v Ira Glass>第二，午餐自助餐大多是油炸食品。丹尼斯神父一样都吃不了，因为他心脏病发作两次后正在康复中，要求吃低脂餐。不过戴夫的妈妈早有准备。

00:04:25.070 --> 00:04:44.330
<v Dave Hill>She sort of like, looks around real quickly and then reaches under the table. And she snuck in a bag of bread and like sandwich meats thinking that we could just make some sandwiches for him at the table. And I'm just horrified.
<v Dave Hill>她快速地看了看周围，然后伸手进桌子下面。她偷带了一袋面包和一些三明治肉片，以为我们可以在桌子上直接为他做三明治。我简直惊呆了。

00:04:44.330 --> 00:04:56.860
<v Ira Glass>Father Dennis tells her very politely, thanks, but no. He doesn't want a sandwich. And eventually the waiters do arrange for him to get some sort of meal. At their table are some very old people, Dave's parents, Dave, and Father Dennis.
<v Ira Glass>丹尼斯神父很有礼貌地告诉她，谢谢，但不用了。他不想吃三明治。最终，服务员还是安排他吃上了一些合适的餐点。在他们的桌子旁，坐着一些非常老的老人、戴夫的父母、戴夫和丹尼斯神父。

00:04:56.860 --> 00:05:03.640
<v Ira Glass>There's no drinking, which Dave says might have really helped things. And after a while, Dave and Father Dennis have run out of small talk.
<v Ira Glass>没有酒，戴夫说如果能喝点酒，情况可能会好转。过了一会儿，戴夫和丹尼斯神父已经无话可说了。

00:05:03.640 --> 00:05:41.460
<v Dave Hill>So it's getting quiet and we're sitting next to each other. At some point, Father Dennis turns to me and he's sort of like, you know, I don't know. Normally I understand why I'm somewhere. If I'm invited somewhere I know why. But I guess I don't know. I don't know why I'm here. I'm really confused. I don't feel any connection to this. And I was just like, what? What do you mean? I thought you wanted to be here. I thought that was the whole thing. That's why I'm here. I thought you wanted to be here.
<v Dave Hill>现场变得安静，我们并排坐着。过了一会儿，丹尼斯神父转向我说，你知道，我不知道。通常我知道自己为什么会在某个地方。如果我受邀参加某个活动，我知道原因。但我猜我不知道。我不知道我为什么会在这儿。我真的很困惑。我感觉不到与这里有任何联系。我当时就说，什么？你什么意思？我以为你想来这里。我以为整件事就是这样。这就是我为什么在这儿的原因。我以为你想来。

00:05:41.460 --> 00:06:04.280
<v Dave Hill>And he says, no. Your mother told me that you wanted to be here and that you really wanted me to go. So I thought that I should do it for you and your mom. And we're just sitting there, like oh gosh. We've been just duped by what I thought was a really nice lady.
<v Dave Hill>他说，不。你妈妈告诉我你想来，而且你非常希望我陪你去。所以我以为我应该为你和你妈这么做。我们当时就坐在那儿，心想，天哪，我们被那个我一直以为是很善良的女士给骗了。

00:06:04.280 --> 00:06:21.050
<v Dave Hill>My mother just tricked us into this.
<v Dave Hill>我妈妈把我们给耍了。

00:06:21.050 --> 00:06:24.130
<v Ira Glass>Did you ask your mom afterwards about the whole thing?
<v Ira Glass>事后你问你妈关于这件事了吗？

00:06:24.130 --> 00:06:24.810
<v Dave Hill>Yeah.
<v Dave Hill>问了。

00:06:24.810 --> 00:06:30.690
<v Ira Glass>And did she explain what she was hoping was going to happen once she got you and Father Dennis together?
<v Ira Glass>她有没有解释她把你和丹尼斯神父聚在一起是想达成什么目的？

00:06:30.690 --> 00:06:52.350
<v Dave Hill>No, she just kept saying like, oh, I just thought it would be a really nice time for you. For both of you. She was very invasive about it. And I confronted her saying, do you think like that I'm going to hang out with his priest and then I'm going to want to become a priest? And she's like, no.
<v Dave Hill>没有，她只是一直说，哦，我只是觉得这对你们来说会是很棒的一段时光。对你们两个都是。她在这方面非常强人所难。我质问她说，你是不是觉得我会跟这个神父待在一起，然后就想去当神父了？她说，没有。

00:06:52.350 --> 00:06:54.460
<v Dave Hill>She wouldn't admit to that, and that's maybe--
<v Dave Hill>她不承认，这也许——

00:06:54.460 --> 00:07:02.910
<v Ira Glass>But you think that's what it might be? Like in the back of her mind she's just like, maybe you're single, you're in your late 20s. Maybe you should be a priest?
<v Ira Glass>但你觉得那可能是她的想法吗？就像在她潜意识里，她觉得，也许你单身，快三十岁了，也许你应该去当神父？

00:07:02.910 --> 00:07:20.260
<v Dave Hill>Maybe. Or at least have some sort of like priestly influence. I think there was like a bigger thing, but she'll never tell me.
<v Dave Hill>也许吧。或者至少会受到某种神职人员的影响。我觉得背后还有更大的原因，但她永远不会告诉我。

00:07:20.260 --> 00:07:34.020
<v Ira Glass>Dave says this was par for the course. His mom often mystified him. And a certain amount of her parenting, of any parenting really, is putting your kids into situations you know are going to be good for them whether they like it or not. Or even, whether they understand it.
<v Ira Glass>戴夫说这是常态。他妈妈经常让他感到困惑。某种程度上的育儿，或者说任何育儿方式，就是把你的孩子带入你认为对他们有益的环境中，不管他们喜不喜欢，甚至不管他们明不明白。

00:07:34.020 --> 00:07:44.180
<v Dave Hill>There's been a number of things over the years that I just could not wrap my head around why she was making me do it. And I really thought she was torturing me.
<v Dave Hill>多年来，有很多事情我都无法理解她为什么要逼我做。我真的觉得她在折磨我。

00:07:44.180 --> 00:07:46.360
<v Ira Glass>Like what?
<v Ira Glass>比如什么？

00:07:46.360 --> 00:08:33.610
<v Dave Hill>She made me take typing lessons that I could not fathom why. I was like 13-years-old, 14-years-old. It just seemed just random, like here's something that I'll hate. I'll just make him do it. And like, now, literally like it didn't even hit me until like the last few years when I realized that a huge part of everyday for me, I'm typing all the time. I remember seeing that one day and be like, oh my God. That's why she made me endure that horrible summer of typing lessons. Literally, 20 years later I figured it out why she did that to me.
<v Dave Hill>她逼我上打字课，我完全无法理解原因。我当时大概13、14岁。这看起来很随机，就像这里有件我会讨厌的事，她就非要让我做。而现在，真的，直到最近几年我才意识到，我每天的大部分时间都在不停地打字。我记得有一天意识到这一点时，我说，噢，我的天哪。这就是她让我忍受那个可怕的暑假打字课的原因。真的，20年后我才弄明白她为什么要那么对我。

00:08:33.610 --> 00:08:38.840
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ Chicago it's This American Life distributed by Public Radio International. I'm Ira Glass.
<v Ira Glass>这里是芝加哥WBEZ电台，《美国生活》，由公共广播国际公司发行。我是艾拉·格拉斯。

00:08:38.840 --> 00:09:25.120
<v Ira Glass>Today on our show, The Parent Trap, stories of parents doing their best to be good parents and do right by their kids by putting the kids into situation that they believe will be helpful. Our show in two acts today. Act one, Letter Day Saint. David Segal has the story of a mom doing something that you would expect and hope a mom would do, give her kid a little advice. Act two, The Opposite of Tarzan. We are very, very excited to have a brand new story from the amazing team that does the show Radiolab. This is a story about interspecies parenting. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>今天我们的节目主题是“育儿陷阱”，讲述的是父母竭尽全力成为好家长，通过将孩子置于他们认为有帮助的处境中来对孩子负责的故事。我们今天有两幕。第一幕，“书信日圣徒”。大卫·西格尔带来了一位母亲的故事，她做了一件你期望且希望母亲会做的事，给她的孩子提供一点建议。第二幕，“泰山的对立面”。我们非常非常兴奋能从制作《放射实验室》的精彩团队那里带来一个全新的故事。这是一个关于跨物种育儿的故事。请继续收听。

00:09:25.120 --> 00:09:28.030
<v Ira Glass>Act 1, Letter Day Saint.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，书信日圣徒。

00:09:28.030 --> 00:09:44.240
<v Ira Glass>Sometimes a parent can do something kind of extraordinary, really above and beyond what most people do with the best of intentions, just looking out for their kid. And do I even need to say that they can have all kinds of unintended consequences. David Segal has this example.
<v Ira Glass>有时，父母会做出一些相当非凡的事，真正超越了大多数人的所作所为，出发点都是为了孩子好。而且我甚至不需要说，他们可能还会导致各种意想不到的后果。大卫·西格尔就有这样一个例子。

00:09:44.240 --> 00:09:56.710
<v David Segal>In 1991, Elizabeth Gee was dying of cancer. And as she thought about the time she had left to live, she worried about her only child, Rebecca. Then 16 years old. And she discussed her worries with her husband.
<v David Segal>1991年，伊丽莎白·吉死于癌症。当她想到自己余下的时光时，她担心她唯一的孩子，当时16岁的丽贝卡。她和丈夫讨论了她的担忧。

00:09:56.710 --> 00:10:01.370
<v Gordon Gee>She just looked at me one night. I mean, she was hooked up with all of this stuff.
<v Gordon Gee>有一天晚上，她看着我。我是说，她身上连着各种管子。

00:10:01.370 --> 00:10:02.680
<v David Segal>This is Gordon Gee.
<v David Segal>这是戈登·吉。

00:10:02.680 --> 00:10:23.090
<v Gordon Gee>And she just looked at me. She said, you know, I really feel that I need to continue to be an influence on Rebecca's life and continue to be part of her life. She knew she was terminal. She knew that she only had about a month to live. And she made this discrimination that she was going to write letters to her daughter.
<v Gordon Gee>她就那么看着我。她说，你知道，我真的觉得我需要继续对丽贝卡的生活产生影响，并继续成为她生活的一部分。她知道自己身患绝症。她知道自己只剩下一个月的生命了。她做出了一个决定，那就是要给她女儿写信。

00:10:23.090 --> 00:10:51.070
<v David Segal>Specifically, birthday letters. The plan she told Gordon was this. She would write letters that he would send to Rebecca, one per year, every year, on December 4, Rebecca's birthday. The letters would be sealed and for Rebecca's eye's only. There were 13 of them. Each tailored for a specific birthday. Plus a letter for the day of Rebecca's wedding. Rebecca didn't know any of this until the first letter, which she read the day she turned 17.
<v David Segal>具体来说，是生日信。她告诉戈登的计划是：她会写一些信，让他每年在12月4日，也就是丽贝卡的生日那天寄给丽贝卡。信会密封好，只供丽贝卡一个人看。总共写了13封。每一封都针对特定的生日。此外还有一封信是留给她婚礼那天看的。丽贝卡在收到第一封信之前对这一切一无所知，那是她17岁生日那天读到的。

00:10:51.070 --> 00:10:55.830
<v David Segal>To a daughter reeling from the loss of her mother, it was the ultimate birthday present.
<v David Segal>对于一个沉浸在丧母之痛中的女儿来说，这是最好的生日礼物。

00:10:55.830 --> 00:11:04.960
<v Rebecca>She said in one of my first letters that these were a way to connect to her. That all I had to do to connect to her was to open a letter. And then she would be there.
<v Rebecca>她在我的第一封信里说，这些信是与她建立联系的一种方式。我只需要打开一封信，就能与她产生连接。然后她就在那里了。

00:11:04.960 --> 00:11:10.240
<v David Segal>One of the early letters arrived on a birthday at college, when she was feeling particularly isolated.
<v David Segal>其中一封早期的信是在她大学的一个生日送到的，当时她感到格外孤独。

00:11:10.240 --> 00:11:35.330
<v Rebecca>And this letter said basically, you will never be alone. You will never be alone. You will always have me. And I just remember feeling this incredible feeling of peace. It almost felt like getting a letter from someone who was alive.
<v Rebecca>这封信基本上说，你永远不会孤单。你永远不会孤单。你永远会有我。我记得当时感到一种难以置信的平静。感觉就像收到了一位在世之人的信一样。

00:11:35.330 --> 00:11:50.120
<v David Segal>It happened the same way every year. Gordon, a college president for most of his professional life, now at Ohio State University, would overnight a sealed envelope, adding a brief card of his own. And every year, Rebecca went through a birthday letter ritual.
<v David Segal>每年都以同样的方式发生。戈登，他职业生涯中大部分时间担任大学校长，现在在俄亥俄州立大学，会连夜寄出一个密封的信封，并附上他自己的一张简短贺卡。每年，丽贝卡都会经历这个生日信的仪式。

00:11:50.120 --> 00:12:14.090
<v Rebecca>Usually sometime in the afternoon before dinner I'd sit down and open it and read Dad's note. Feel the page, look at her signature. Usually it'd be the first thing. And think, gosh, that's just so amazing that she's gone and I'm looking at her signature and it's saying I love you. And then I'd read through it.
<v Rebecca>通常在午饭后的某个下午，我会坐下来打开它，读爸爸写的便条。摸摸信纸，看看她的签名。通常那是最先触动我的。心想，天哪，她不在了，而我看着她的签名，它在说“我爱你”，这真是太神奇了。然后我会读下去。

00:12:14.090 --> 00:12:24.240
<v David Segal>Each letter was about 2,000 typed words on thick white paper signed by hand, "You're the sweetest girl in the world. Or just, "Love forever, Mommy."
<v David Segal>每一封信大约有2000字，打印在厚厚的白纸上，亲笔签名为“你是世界上最贴心的女孩”，或者只是“永远爱你的妈妈”。

00:12:24.240 --> 00:12:42.520
<v David Segal>Elizabeth told Gordon that she wanted to describe the world as she saw it, and as Rebecca would see it as a young woman. And she does so in the letters with a mix of pep talks, moral instruction, autobiography, and parental affection. Often she focuses on what was happening to her on the age Rebecca would be when she opened each letter.
<v David Segal>伊丽莎白告诉戈登，她想描述她眼中的世界，以及作为一名年轻女性，丽贝卡将会看到的世界。她在信中混合了鼓励、道德指导、自传和父母的关爱。她经常关注丽贝卡读到每一封信时可能会面临的情况。

00:12:42.520 --> 00:12:59.670
<v David Segal>So when Rebecca turned 21, Elizabeth described a dress she owned when she was 21. Quote, "You would not believe what I wore. A black fake fur suit with a pink top and the plastic ping pong earrings. Everyone thought it was fashionable, but I laugh when I think of it."
<v David Segal>所以当丽贝卡21岁时，伊丽莎白描述了她21岁时穿的一件衣服。引用：“你简直无法想象我穿了什么。一件黑色人造皮草套装，配上一件粉色上衣和塑料乒乓球耳环。大家都觉得那很时髦，但现在想起我都会笑。”

00:12:59.670 --> 00:13:16.000
<v David Segal>A lot of the letters are advice on how to live. Quote, "Most children benefit from the simulation of daycare," she writes. Urging Rebecca not to give up her career when she has children. Quote, "You are not a uni-dimensional person. Under that balance, you will be frustrated."
<v David Segal>很多信件都是关于如何生活的建议。引用：“大多数孩子受益于日托的模拟，”她写道。督促丽贝卡在有了孩子后不要放弃职业生涯。引用：“你不是一个单一维度的人。在这种平衡之下，你会感到沮丧。”

00:13:16.000 --> 00:13:29.360
<v David Segal>And her mother had high expectations. In one letter she asks her daughter, "Are you contemplating a dissertation? Interviewing with scientific laboratories or NASA? Traveling to excited places?" Rebecca pushed herself to meet those expectations.
<v David Segal>而且她的母亲对她寄予厚望。在一封信中，她问她的女儿：“你在考虑写博士论文吗？在面试科学实验室或NASA吗？去令人兴奋的地方旅行了吗？”丽贝卡努力去实现这些期望。

00:13:29.360 --> 00:13:39.510
<v David Segal>In college when her friends were out drinking, she stayed in and studied. Other kids might have found this all a little too heavy, Rebecca found it inspiring.
<v David Segal>在大学里，当朋友们出去喝酒时，她留下来学习。其他孩子可能会觉得这一切有点太沉重，丽贝卡却觉得这很激励人。

00:13:39.510 --> 00:13:50.540
<v Rebecca>For example, when I decided I didn't have the confidence to go to medical school and I wasn't going to do it, the letter that year basically said, you need to find ethical expression in your work.
<v Rebecca>例如，当我决定自己没有信心去读医学院并且打算放弃时，那一年收到的那封信基本上说，你需要在你的工作中找到道德表现。

00:13:50.540 --> 00:13:52.590
<v David Segal>She ended up going to medical school.
<v David Segal>她最终还是去了医学院。

00:13:52.590 --> 00:14:14.050
<v Rebecca>There was just no way I could have become a banker in the setting of these letters. I mean not that bankers don't have ethical expression, but for most of college, I think I had this enormous sense of purpose that I had a responsibility to do something meaningful. Both to me and to other people.
<v Rebecca>在这种信件的背景下，我根本不可能成为一名银行家。我的意思是，并不是说银行家没有道德表现，但在大学的大部分时间里，我想我有一种巨大的目标感，觉得我有责任去做一些有意义的事情。对我自己和对别人都是如此。

00:14:14.050 --> 00:14:30.690
<v David Segal>As galvanizing as these letters could be, they could also be upsetting. The first person to fully appreciate how upsetting they were was her father. He was the one who dealt with the fall out. He saw that keeping his promise to his wife meant causing his daughter emotional distress. And on her birthday.
<v David Segal>虽然这些信件可以起到激励作用，但也可能令人不安。第一个完全意识到这些信件有多令人不安的人是她的父亲。他是处理后果的人。他看到，信守对妻子的承诺意味着给女儿带来情感上的痛苦。而且还是在她的生日那天。

00:14:30.690 --> 00:14:45.610
<v Gordon Gee>It was as if I could predict the sunrising. Rebecca would get the letters and then, I'd receive either that night or the next morning, a very tearful telephone call about how much she missed her mom. And I'd have these prolonged telephone calls.
<v Gordon Gee>就好像我可以预测太阳升起一样。丽贝卡会收到信，然后在那天晚上或第二天早上，我就会接到一个非常伤心的电话，诉说她有多想念妈妈。我就会接到这些漫长的电话。

00:14:45.610 --> 00:14:47.030
<v David Segal>What sort of things would you talk about?
<v David Segal>你们会聊些什么？

00:14:47.030 --> 00:15:12.850
<v Gordon Gee>Well, I mean, she'd just say she's sad. You know, very sad. I wish my mother had been able to live. And been able to be here for me when I needed her. And been able to go shopping with me and all the kinds of things that I think any young woman would love to have her mother do. And you can imagine, that was very difficult for me.
<v Gordon Gee>嗯，我的意思是，她只会说她很伤心。你知道，非常伤心。我希望我妈妈能活下来。希望在我需要她的时候她能在身边。希望她能陪我逛街，能做任何我认为任何年轻女性都希望母亲做的事情。你可以想象，这对我很困难。

00:15:12.850 --> 00:15:29.020
<v David Segal>Toward the end of her college years, the letters were difficult for Rebecca for another reason. One of her mother's regular theme, actually you could say it was the dominant theme, was religious faith. The Gee's are Mormons and to her mother, staying close to the church was essential.
<v David Segal>在大学快结束时，这些信件对丽贝卡来说因为另一个原因而变得困难。她母亲经常谈论的主题，实际上你可以说是主导主题，是宗教信仰。吉家是摩门教徒，对她母亲来说，保持与教会的亲近至关重要。

00:15:29.020 --> 00:15:44.130
<v Rebecca>The most important thing for her was that I be a Mormon woman in the way that she wanted me to be. Which was a woman that went to the temple. A woman who married a Mormon man. A woman who believed all of the Mormon theology.
<v Rebecca>对她来说最重要的事情就是，我成为她所期望的那种摩门教女性。也就是一个去圣殿的女性。一个嫁给摩门教男性的女性。一个相信所有摩门教神学的女性。

00:15:44.130 --> 00:16:06.340
<v David Segal>By the time Rebecca was a senior in college, she was moving away from the church. So she worried a lot about disappointing her mother, who in the letters was constantly imploring her to stay. Quote, "You must always have the goal of going to the temple. For there you receive great gifts. It is in the temple that we are joined in eternal bonds and powers that will unite us in the world's beyond."
<v David Segal>当丽贝卡大学四年级时，她正逐渐疏远教会。所以她很担心会让母亲失望，因为母亲在信中不断恳求她留下来。引用：“你一定要始终以去圣殿为目标。在那里你会得到巨大的馈赠。正是在圣殿里，我们在永恒的纽带和力量中结合，这将把我们在来世团结在一起。”

00:16:06.340 --> 00:16:17.230
<v David Segal>In another letter she wrote, "You will make good life choices. I know you will. But no matter your choice, never lose sight of the temple for me, please."
<v David Segal>在另一封信中她写道：“你会做出好的人生选择。我知道你会的。但无论你如何选择，请不要为了我而忽略圣殿，求你了。”

00:16:17.230 --> 00:17:07.780
<v Rebecca>By age 21, after a few years of getting these letters and going through a process where I realized this right not going to be in my life, sometimes I would feel angry. And I remember at age 21 thinking, man, do I have to open up this one this year? This is tough. I don't want to do it. And my friend saying, well, why don't you wait? We'll go out to dinner. Maybe open it up tomorrow. And I felt guilty. And I remember feeling like if I didn't open it up I would really disappoint her. And yet, then I open it up and it's all about, I hope you marry a Mormon man and I hope you go to the temple. And if you don't go to the temple, you won't go to heaven. You're not going to see me. And I'm not doing it. And that's a pretty hard thing to hear on your birthday.
<v Rebecca>到了21岁，在收到这些信好几年并经历了意识到我的人生中不再有这个信仰的过程后，有时我会感到愤怒。我记得21岁时想，天哪，今年我非得打开这一封吗？这太难了。我不想这么做。我的朋友说，好吧，那你为什么不等等呢？我们出去吃晚饭。也许明天再打开。我感到内疚。我记得当时觉得如果我不打开它，我就会让她非常失望。然而，当我打开它时，里面全都是，我希望你嫁给一个摩门教男人，我希望你去圣殿。如果你不去圣殿，你就去不了天堂。你就见不到我了。我做不到这一点。在生日那天听到这些真的很痛苦。

00:17:07.780 --> 00:17:40.620
<v David Segal>Gordon was devout, but he could listen to Rebecca's reasons for leaving the church and came to accept them. Her mother couldn't do that. And in the three-way conversation that is part of any relationship of child, mother, and father, the voice of Rebecca's mother always spoke the loudest. It might seem strange that the deceased parent had the most sway, but her mother's opinion was fixed. There's no arguing with her. That haunted Rebecca. And while Gordon found himself reassuring his daughter, the comforts he offered were always trumped by Rebecca's memory of her mother.
<v David Segal>戈登是一个虔诚的人，但他能倾听丽贝卡离开教会的理由，并开始接受它们。她的母亲做不到。在任何孩子、母亲和父亲的关系对话中，丽贝卡母亲的声音总是最大的。已故的父母对孩子有最大的影响力似乎很奇怪，但她母亲的观点是固定的。没法跟她争论。这困扰着丽贝卡。虽然戈登发现自己在安抚女儿，但他提供的安慰总是被丽贝卡对母亲的记忆所掩盖。

00:17:40.620 --> 00:18:06.660
<v Gordon Gee>It's difficult to compete with a dead spouse or in this sense, a dead mother. Because I'm here and I have all of the frailties of who I am. And over time, obviously her mother became very iconic. In many ways, very perfect. And clearly, could do no wrong. And I mean I never did say this to her, although I've joked with her a couple times. You know, who am I, chopped liver?
<v Gordon Gee>与已故的配偶，或者在这种意义上，已故的母亲竞争是很困难的。因为我就在这里，我有我所有的软弱。随着时间的推移，她的母亲显然变得非常有偶像色彩。在很多方面，非常完美。显然，她不可能做错任何事。我的意思是，我从没对她说出口过，尽管我跟她开过几次玩笑。你知道，我是谁，不重要吗？

00:18:06.660 --> 00:18:22.610
<v David Segal>Because the letters were written exclusively for Rebecca, Gordon thought it was inappropriate to ask too many questions about them. For the same reason, Rebecca never volunteered to share them. Father and daughter were just following a set of instructions. As outdated and as trying as those instructions were.
<v David Segal>因为这些信是专门为丽贝卡写的，戈登认为问太多问题是不合适的。同样的原因，丽贝卡也从没主动分享过。父女俩只是遵循了一套指令。尽管这些指令是如此过时且令人煎熬。

00:18:22.610 --> 00:18:31.600
<v David Segal>Over the years, as he dealt with his daughter's anguish, Gordon tossed out some hints the perhaps it was a bad idea for him to lob these annual grief bombs into her life.
<v David Segal>多年来，当他应对女儿的痛苦时，戈登抛出了一些暗示，觉得他把这些年度悲伤炸弹投进她的生活可能是一个糟糕的主意。

00:18:31.600 --> 00:19:00.140
<v Gordon Gee>I did on several occasions, I just said, you know, you have to remember that your mother was very ill. And that she loved you very much. But I'm not quite certain how healthy this is to have these letters and I said that to her directly. And I've said that to her on several occasions. And because it was very hard on me. It would really break my heart when I would hear her be so sad.
<v Gordon Gee>我有好几次直接对她说，你知道，你必须记住你母亲病得很重。她非常爱你。但我不太确定拥有这些信件对健康是否有益。这对我很艰难。当我听到她那么伤心时，真的会让我心碎。

00:19:00.140 --> 00:19:15.890
<v David Segal>But Rebecca never seemed to pick up on her father's hints, or his misgivings. As painful as the letters were, she felt as though she had no choice but to read them carefully. So Gordon sent them all, though he was conflicted about it enough that he actually delegated the FedExing part to his secretary.
<v David Segal>但丽贝卡似乎从未领会她父亲的暗示或忧虑。尽管这些信件很痛苦，但她觉得她别无选择，只能仔细阅读它们。所以戈登把它们全寄了，尽管他内心很矛盾，以至于他实际上把联邦快递的任务委派给了秘书。

00:19:15.890 --> 00:19:40.040
<v David Segal>All of this letter related dread culminated with Rebecca's wedding, which happened in 2006 in an Episcopal church. But Rebecca and Gordon suspected the letter Elizabeth wrote for Rebecca's wedding would contain her longest course yet on the importance of marrying in a Mormon temple. Which is why both father and daughter told me they were secretly relieved when the letter, which Gordon's secretary says she sent, vanished in the FedEx system.
<v David Segal>所有这些与信件相关的恐惧在丽贝卡的婚礼上达到了顶峰，婚礼发生在2006年的一座圣公会教堂。但丽贝卡和戈登怀疑伊丽莎白为婚礼写的信会包含她最长的一堂关于在摩门教圣殿结婚重要性的课程。这就是为什么父女俩都告诉我，当那封由戈登秘书说她寄出的信在联邦快递系统中消失时，他们暗暗松了一口气。

00:19:40.040 --> 00:20:04.840
<v Rebecca>I remember thinking, if there is one for my wedding, I definitely don't want to open it on my wedding day. Because I really want my wedding day to be happy. And I don't want to sit, I don't want to cry. That day in particular, didn't want the letter to be a part of it.
<v Rebecca>我记得当时想，如果婚礼有一封信，我绝对不想在婚礼当天打开。因为我真的希望我的婚礼当天是快乐的。我不想坐着，不想哭。那天尤其如此，我不希望这封信成为婚礼的一部分。

00:20:04.840 --> 00:20:10.250
<v David Segal>Because all the birthday letters had been sent, the wedding letter would have been the final letter in the series.
<v David Segal>因为所有的生日信都已经寄出，这封婚礼信将是系列中的最后一封。

00:20:10.250 --> 00:20:15.010
<v David Segal>Was there part of you when the letters ran out that felt relief?
<v David Segal>当信件寄完时，你内心有没有一种如释重负的感觉？

00:20:15.010 --> 00:20:28.720
<v Gordon Gee>Yeah, I think so. I think that without a doubt, it was an opportunity to move on. On the other level, this was the last kind of visible written, tangible connection that she had with her mother.
<v Gordon Gee>有，我想是的。毫无疑问，这是一个继续前进的机会。在另一个层面上，这是她与母亲之间最后一种可见、书面、有形的联系。

00:20:28.720 --> 00:20:31.150
<v David Segal>It's almost like another death?
<v David Segal>这几乎就像是又一次死亡？

00:20:31.150 --> 00:20:35.750
<v Gordon Gee>Right. And I think it was to her in many ways.
<v Gordon Gee>对。而且我认为在很多方面对她来说确实如此。

00:20:35.750 --> 00:20:59.570
<v David Segal>But it was also a new beginning. For the first time, Rebecca began living a life that wasn't shadowed by a looming reminder of her mother's unmet hopes. Rebecca had become a physician. And she married a doctor named Allan Moore, whom she had met in residency. It was a very happy marriage and it ended tragically, a mere 18 months after it started when the couple was hit by an SUV while riding a Vespa.
<v David Segal>但这也是一个新的开始。丽贝卡第一次开始过上不再被母亲未实现的愿望的阴影所笼罩的生活。丽贝卡成为了一名医生。她嫁给了一位名叫艾伦·摩尔的医生，他们是在住院实习期间认识的。这是一段非常幸福的婚姻，但在开始仅18个月后就悲剧性地结束了，当时夫妇俩在骑Vespa摩托车时被一辆SUV撞倒。

00:20:59.570 --> 00:21:15.690
<v David Segal>Rebecca was severely injured and Allan was killed. For the second time in her life, Rebecca was forced to cope with a devastating loss. But this time was different. She spent her months of physical rehab contemplating and writing about her memories of Allan.
<v David Segal>丽贝卡受了重伤，艾伦去世了。丽贝卡一生中第二次被迫面对毁灭性的损失。但这次不同了。她在身体康复的几个月里沉思并写下了关于艾伦的记忆。

00:21:15.690 --> 00:21:43.440
<v Rebecca>And I remember thinking to myself, this is how it should be when you grieve for someone. You should remember the beautiful times, the things you shared. You should celebrate that person, but you shouldn't be dragged back into the grave with them every year. Why did she do this to me?
<v Rebecca>我记得当时想，当你为某人哀悼时，这就是应该的方式。你应该记住美好的时光，你们分享的一切。你应该庆祝那个人，但不应该每年都被拖回坟墓里。她为什么要对我这样做？

00:21:43.440 --> 00:21:51.860
<v David Segal>Rebecca says a year and a half after her husband's death, she feels like she's farther along in her grieving for him than she was 10 years after her mother died.
<v David Segal>丽贝卡说，在丈夫去世一年半后，她觉得自己在为他哀悼的过程中比她母亲去世10年后走得更远。

00:21:51.860 --> 00:22:14.660
<v Rebecca>I mourned my husband's loss, Allan's loss. I love him, but I'm moving on with my life as he would want me to. I think to have letters from him for my birthdays or a yearly, would make it harder for me to move on.
<v Rebecca>我哀悼我丈夫的失去，艾伦的失去。我爱他，但我像他所希望的那样，正在继续我的生活。我认为如果每年生日都收到他的信，会让我更难继续前进。

00:22:14.660 --> 00:22:22.810
<v David Segal>At the same time, the letters her mother sent were so essential in forming the person that Rebecca became, it's hard for her to imagine growing up without them.
<v David Segal>与此同时，她母亲寄来的信对塑造现在的丽贝卡至关重要，她很难想象没有它们会怎样长大。

00:22:22.810 --> 00:22:46.820
<v Rebecca>In the end, would I have wanted a life without these? I can't say that because they made me feel incredibly loved. They made me know how much she valued her time on this earth with me. I got to hear that. I got to hear that after she died.
<v Rebecca>最后，我是否希望拥有没有这些信的生活？我不能那么说，因为它们让我感到被深深地爱着。它们让我知道她在地球上与我共度的时光对她来说有多么珍贵。我听到了这些。我在她去世后听到了这些。

00:22:46.820 --> 00:23:20.120
<v David Segal>Recently, Rebecca has started to grasp the hardships the letters imposed on her dad. And she thinks it would've been better if she had shared the letters with her father in real time, even if that went against her mother's wishes. This surely wasn't Elizabeth's intention, but when she wrote those letter, she left behind the bricks for a wall that kept her husband and her daughter apart, at least when the subject came to Elizabeth. Along with instructions that made discussing that wall very difficult. It was a plan that neither Rebecca nor Gordon ever consented to. And now, very gingerly, the wall is being dismantled.
<v David Segal>最近，丽贝卡开始理解这些信件给她父亲带来的困难。她认为，如果她能实时与父亲分享这些信件会更好，即使这违背了母亲的意愿。这当然不是伊丽莎白的初衷，但当她写那些信时，她留下了一堵墙的砖块，让丈夫和女儿之间产生了隔阂，至少在涉及伊丽莎白的问题上是这样。而且这些指令让讨论那堵墙变得非常困难。这是一个丽贝卡和戈登从未同意过的计划。现在，非常小心地，那堵墙正在被拆除。

00:23:20.120 --> 00:23:37.170
<v David Segal>A months ago Rebecca sent her Dad a copy of one of the letters from her 24th birthday. Much of which is about Gordon's strengths as a dad. She has yet to share any of the others. And during our interview, Gordon joked that he was a little jealous that I got to read the letters before he did.
<v David Segal>几个月前，丽贝卡把她24岁生日时收到的一封信复印件寄给了父亲。信的大部分内容是关于戈登作为父亲的优点。她还没分享过其他的信。在我们的采访中，戈登开玩笑说，他有点嫉妒我竟然比他先读到了这些信。

00:23:37.170 --> 00:23:52.840
<v David Segal>But a dying wish has a momentum all its own. And though the letters are now locked in a safe in Gordon's home, he still hasn't looked at them. And Rebecca still hasn't asked him to.
<v David Segal>但遗愿有其自身的力量。虽然信件现在锁在戈登家里的保险箱里，但他还是没看它们。丽贝卡也还没要求他看。

