<v Robert Krulwich>你真的带在身边吗？

00:26:44.150 --> 00:26:58.770
<v Charles Siebert>Hold on. It's called Lucy- Growing Up Human : A Chimpanzee Daughter in a Psychotherapist's Family by Maurice K. Temerlin.
<v Charles Siebert>等一下。书名叫《露西——成长为人：心理治疗师家庭中的黑猩猩女儿》，作者是莫里斯·K·特默林。

00:26:58.770 --> 00:27:01.250
<v Jad Abumrad>Maurice K. Temerlin, he is the psychotherapist.
<v Jad Abumrad>莫里斯·K·特默林，他就是那位心理治疗师。

00:27:01.250 --> 00:27:02.550
<v Charles Siebert>He's a psychotherapist.
<v Charles Siebert>他是位心理治疗师。

00:27:02.550 --> 00:27:21.170
<v Jad Abumrad>And he's also the dad in this story. And his wife, Jane, who's a social worker, she's the mom. Now the thing to know was that, especially for Maurice Temerlin, this was more than just adopting a baby champ. This was an experiment. He wanted to know, given the right upbringing, how human could Lucy he become?
<v Jad Abumrad>他也是这个故事里的爸爸。他的妻子简是一位社会工作者，她是妈妈。需要知道的是，特别是对莫里斯·特默林来说，这不仅仅是领养了一只黑猩猩宝宝。这是一项实验。他想知道，在适当的教养下，露西能变得多像人类？

00:27:21.170 --> 00:27:27.350
<v Charles Siebert>What he says early on in this book, "Would she learn to love us and perhaps--
<v Charles Siebert>他在书的开头说，“她会学会爱我们，也许——

00:27:27.350 --> 00:27:32.220
<v David Garland>--have other human emotions as well? Would she be well-behaved, rebellious.
<v David Garland>——还会产生其他人类情感吗？她会表现良好、叛逆吗？

00:27:32.220 --> 00:27:33.450
<v Charles Siebert>Intelligent or stupid?
<v Charles Siebert>聪明还是愚蠢？

00:27:33.450 --> 00:27:36.010
<v David Garland>What about sex?
<v David Garland>性生活呢？

00:27:36.010 --> 00:27:41.210
<v Jad Abumrad>Maurice Temerlin actually died in 1989, but these are his words read by radio host, David Garland.
<v Jad Abumrad>莫里斯·特默林实际上于1989年去世，但这些话是由电台主持人戴维·加兰朗读的。

00:27:41.210 --> 00:27:46.170
<v David Garland>Would she mother her offspring? Could she learn to talk? How intelligent might she be?
<v David Garland>她会像母亲一样对待她的后代吗？她能学会说话吗？她会有多聪明？

00:27:46.170 --> 00:27:47.560
<v Jad Abumrad>And so how did they her?
<v Jad Abumrad>那么他们是怎么得到她的？

00:27:47.560 --> 00:27:52.590
<v Robert Krulwich>He says that he and his wife, Jane, made all the arrangements. Went and got the chimp--
<v Robert Krulwich>他说他和妻子简做好了所有安排。去把这只黑猩猩带回来——

00:27:52.590 --> 00:27:53.890
<v David Garland>--from the day the infant was born.
<v David Garland>——从婴儿出生的那天起。

00:27:53.890 --> 00:27:56.110
<v Robert Krulwich>The mother was anesthetized.
<v Robert Krulwich>母亲被麻醉了。

00:27:56.110 --> 00:28:11.520
<v David Garland>In the early morning of her second day, Jane fed the mother a Coca-Cola, which had been spiked with phencyclidine, a drug which puts chimpanzees into a deep, pleasant sleep.
<v David Garland>在她出生第二天的清晨，简给母亲喂了一杯可口可乐，里面混入了苯环己哌啶，这是一种能让黑猩猩进入深度、舒适睡眠的药物。

00:28:11.520 --> 00:28:14.280
<v Jad Abumrad>And the baby was taken away.
<v Jad Abumrad>婴儿被带走了。

00:28:14.280 --> 00:28:28.510
<v David Garland>Jane named her Lucy and brought her home on a commercial airline, carried in a bassinet, her face covered with a lacy blanket. We were blissfully unaware of the complexities we were creating on the day Lucy came home.
<v David Garland>简给她取名露西，通过商业航空公司把她带回家，装在一个摇篮里，脸上盖着一条蕾丝毯子。当露西回家的那天，我们全然不知道我们正在制造复杂的事情。

00:28:28.510 --> 00:28:30.440
<v Robert Krulwich>So the baby was a day or two old?
<v Robert Krulwich>所以婴儿才一两天大？

00:28:30.440 --> 00:28:31.420
<v Charles Siebert>Just two days old.
<v Charles Siebert>只有两天大。

00:28:31.420 --> 00:28:32.460
<v Robert Krulwich>So it wasn't weened?
<v Robert Krulwich>所以还没断奶？

00:28:32.460 --> 00:28:34.550
<v Charles Siebert>No, and that was part of the experiment.
<v Charles Siebert>不，这也是实验的一部分。

00:28:34.550 --> 00:28:35.100
<v Robert Krulwich>They bottle feed her.
<v Robert Krulwich>他们用奶瓶喂她。

00:28:35.100 --> 00:28:35.380
<v Charles Siebert>Yeah.
<v Charles Siebert>是的。

00:28:35.380 --> 00:28:47.690
<v David Garland>She quickly learned to hold her own bottle. At two months, her eyes would focus. At three months, she was trying to climb out of her crib to go to people. And at six months, she was pretty mobile on all four limbs.
<v David Garland>她很快学会了自己拿着奶瓶。两个月大时，她的眼睛能聚焦了。三个月大时，她试图爬出婴儿床去找人。六个月大时，她的四肢移动已经非常灵活了。

00:28:47.690 --> 00:28:51.780
<v Jad Abumrad>The memoir goes on. By the time she was about a year old--
<v Jad Abumrad>回忆录继续写道。当她大约一岁大时——

00:28:51.780 --> 00:28:53.710
<v David Garland>She was eating at the table with us.
<v David Garland>她和我们一起在餐桌上吃饭。

00:28:53.710 --> 00:28:54.620
<v Jad Abumrad>Forks, spoons, knives.
<v Jad Abumrad>叉子、勺子、刀子。

00:28:54.620 --> 00:28:57.820
<v David Garland>She would see us using silverware and immediately dos o herself.
<v David Garland>她看到我们使用银器，立刻也会自己使用。

00:28:57.820 --> 00:28:59.860
<v Jad Abumrad>She began to dress herself in skirts.
<v Jad Abumrad>她开始穿裙子。

00:28:59.860 --> 00:29:09.460
<v David Garland>Shw would often grab my hand, pull me to my feet and beg me to chase her. Always looking back to see that Daddy was not too far behind.
<v David Garland>她经常抓住我的手，拉我站起来，求我追她。总是回头看看爸爸是不是离得不远。

00:29:09.460 --> 00:29:16.970
<v Charles Siebert>You know, he really went at this with this sort of full bore earnestness. You know, when he calls her his darling daughter--
<v Charles Siebert>你知道，他确实以一种非常认真、全神贯注的态度对待这件事。你知道，当他称她为宝贝女儿时——

00:29:16.970 --> 00:29:19.290
<v David Garland>I took great pride in my daughter's achievements.
<v David Garland>我为女儿取得的成就感到非常自豪。

00:29:19.290 --> 00:29:22.890
<v Charles Siebert>He does feel like a real parent to Lucy.
<v Charles Siebert>他确实觉得自己是露西真正的父母。

00:29:22.890 --> 00:29:26.080
<v David Garland>She was so responsive to being looked at, held, and stroked.
<v David Garland>她对被注视、被拥抱和被抚摸非常敏感。

00:29:26.080 --> 00:29:32.270
<v Charles Siebert>But he's also, make no mistake, treating this as a very intense cutting edge experiment.
<v Charles Siebert>但请不要搞错，他也把这当作一个非常激烈、前沿的实验来对待。

00:29:32.270 --> 00:29:42.540
<v Jad Abumrad>The next phase of the experiment, which occupies a good deal of the book, involve one of those talents that we thought used to only be limited to us: language.
<v Jad Abumrad>实验的下一阶段，占据了书的大部分内容，涉及到我们以前认为只有我们才有的那种天赋：语言。

00:29:42.540 --> 00:29:43.480
<v Roger Fouts>OK.
<v Roger Fouts>好的。

00:29:43.480 --> 00:29:44.670
<v Jad Abumrad>Can you introduce yourself, please?
<v Jad Abumrad>你能自我介绍一下吗？

00:29:44.670 --> 00:29:52.960
<v Roger Fouts>OK, my name is Roger Fouts. I'm a professor of psychology and I've worked with chimpanzees since 1967.
<v Roger Fouts>好的，我叫罗杰·福茨。我是一名心理学教授，从1967年开始研究黑猩猩。

00:29:52.960 --> 00:29:58.410
<v Jad Abumrad>Roger Fouts was called in by Maurice Temerlin to address one of the crucial questions of the experiment.
<v Jad Abumrad>罗杰·福茨被莫里斯·特默林请来解决实验中一个关键问题。

00:29:58.410 --> 00:29:59.770
<v David Garland>Could she learn to talk?
<v David Garland>她能学会说话吗？

