00:40:58.250 --> 00:40:59.650
<v Janice Carter>And crossed the Gambia River.
<v Janice Carter>穿过了冈比亚河。

00:40:59.650 --> 00:41:01.840
<v Jad Abumrad>And then made their way to a nature reserve.
<v Jad Abumrad>然后去了自然保护区。

00:41:01.840 --> 00:41:02.740
<v Janice Carter>A nature reserve.
<v Janice Carter>自然保护区。

00:41:02.740 --> 00:41:04.810
<v Jad Abumrad>Which was basically just a bunch of big cages.
<v Jad Abumrad>那里基本上只是一堆大笼子。

00:41:04.810 --> 00:41:06.910
<v Janice Carter>Really large enclosures there--
<v Janice Carter>里面有非常大的围栏——

00:41:06.910 --> 00:41:27.270
<v Jad Abumrad>Sitting right outside in the jungle. So they get there, coax Lucy into one of these cases, say their goodbyes for the night, and they leave her to spend her very first night alone outdoors.
<v Jad Abumrad>坐落在丛林外面。所以他们到了那里，把露西哄进其中一个笼子里，说了晚安，然后留下她一个人在户外度过第一个夜晚。

00:41:27.270 --> 00:41:39.400
<v Jad Abumrad>After a few weeks, Maurice and Jane Temerlin decided to leave. And the plan was that Janice, for just a little while, would stay behind. To help Lucy with the transition.
<v Jad Abumrad>几周后，莫里斯和简·特默林决定离开。计划是珍妮丝留下来一段时间，帮助露西过渡。

00:41:39.400 --> 00:41:51.590
<v Janice Carter>She started to lose her hair and get skin infections. I wasn't happy being there either. I hated it.
<v Janice Carter>她开始脱发并出现皮肤感染。我留在那儿也不开心。我讨厌那儿。

00:41:51.590 --> 00:41:54.170
<v Jad Abumrad>How long did you think you would be staying there?
<v Jad Abumrad>你以为你会留多久？

00:41:54.170 --> 00:41:55.330
<v Janice Carter>Three weeks.
<v Janice Carter>三周。

00:41:55.330 --> 00:42:05.590
<v Jad Abumrad>Three weeks. Wow. And so were saying that Janice Carter has actually never left.
<v Jad Abumrad>三周。哇。所以你是说珍妮丝·卡特实际上再也没离开过。

00:42:05.590 --> 00:42:12.020
<v Janice Carter>At the end of those three weeks, there was just no way that I could leave Lucy.
<v Janice Carter>三周结束时，我根本没法离开露西。

00:42:12.020 --> 00:42:38.610
<v Jad Abumrad>The weeks turn into months, and then into a year. And still, Lucy's stressed out. She's not eating, her hair is falling out. And by this point, a whole nother group of chimps shows up at this nature reserve. These were former captives like Lucy, and they start to deteriorate as well. So Janice decides what she needs to do is change locations. So she takes Lucy and all these other chimps to this abandoned island that she'd found.
<v Jad Abumrad>几周变成了几个月，然后变成了一年。露西依然压力重重。她不吃东西，毛发在脱落。此时，另一群黑猩猩出现在这个自然保护区。这些也是像露西一样的曾经被囚禁的动物，它们也开始恶化。所以珍妮丝决定她需要换个地点。她带着露西和所有这些黑猩猩去了她发现的一个荒岛。

00:42:38.610 --> 00:42:40.580
<v Janice Carter>It's a long narrow island.
<v Janice Carter>这是一个又长又窄的岛。

00:42:40.580 --> 00:42:41.540
<v Jad Abumrad>This is in the Gambia River.
<v Jad Abumrad>在冈比亚河里。

00:42:41.540 --> 00:42:46.350
<v Janice Carter>It's a mile wide at its widest point. Very thick screen forest.
<v Janice Carter>最宽处只有一英里。森林非常茂密。

00:42:46.350 --> 00:43:01.680
<v Robert Krulwich>And the idea here was that you would release them and they would be able to do whatever in the island and learn how to climb trees, and learn how to forage, and learn how to establish relationships with each other? Was that the notion?
<v Robert Krulwich>这里的想法是释放它们，它们就能在岛上做任何事，学会爬树、学会觅食、学会建立彼此之间的关系？这就是最初的想法吗？

00:43:01.680 --> 00:43:12.430
<v Janice Carter>Yeah, in a nutshell. And you would think that if you gave them freedom, they would just jump for joy and that's the last chapter of the book.
<v Janice Carter>是的，简而言之。你会以为如果你给它们自由，它们会跳得很高兴，那将是书的最后一章。

00:43:12.430 --> 00:43:30.670
<v Jad Abumrad>But it's not what happened. She says that when Lucy and the other chimps got to the island and she let them loose, they clung to her. During the day she'd walk them around the island and point out of them, here are the fruits you should be eating. These are the leaves you should be eating. They weren't interested in any of that stuff.
<v Jad Abumrad>但这并没有发生。她说当露西和其他黑猩猩到达岛上时，她放它们出去，它们却紧紧缠着她。白天她带它们绕岛走，指点它们，这是你们应该吃的果子。这是你们应该吃的叶子。它们对这些东西一点兴趣都没有。

00:43:30.670 --> 00:43:31.310
<v Janice Carter>Oh no.
<v Janice Carter>哦，不。

00:43:31.310 --> 00:43:34.900
<v David Segal>They were actually more interested in her stuff. Which was what they were used to.
<v David Segal>它们实际上对她的东西更感兴趣。因为它们习惯了那些。

00:43:34.900 --> 00:43:53.520
<v Janice Carter>I had human objects and tools that I needed for my own survival and they wanted to use them. Like when I would cook or brush my teeth or take a bath, or anything that I wanted to do, they wanted to be doing it with me.
<v Janice Carter>我有一些为了生存所需的人类物品和工具，它们想使用这些。就像我做饭、刷牙或洗澡，或者我想做的任何事，它们都想和我一起做。

00:43:53.520 --> 00:44:02.680
<v Jad Abumrad>Janice figured the only way this was going to work is if she could somehow keep the chimps away from her and her tools. And so here's where she does something really radical.
<v Jad Abumrad>珍妮丝想，唯一可行的方法是如果她能以某种方式让黑猩猩远离她和她的工具。所以在这里她做了一件非常激进的事情。

00:44:02.680 --> 00:44:17.710
<v Jad Abumrad>She had run into a couple of British army officers who were passing through the Gambi on some kind of wilderness training thing. And she somehow convinced them to build her a cage, a giant metal industrial cage. Then to fly it over to her island.
<v Jad Abumrad>她遇到了几个英国军官，他们正经过冈比亚进行某种野外训练。她不知怎么说服他们为她造了一个笼子，一个巨大的金属工业笼子。然后用飞机把它运到岛上。

00:44:17.710 --> 00:44:18.750
<v Janice Carter>In a helicopter.
<v Janice Carter>用直升机。

00:44:18.750 --> 00:44:27.210
<v Jad Abumrad>And drop it thunk, right in the center. The thing about this cage is that it wasn't for the chimps. It was for her.
<v Jad Abumrad>把它砰地一声扔在正中心。这个笼子的特别之处在于它不是为了关黑猩猩的。是为了关她自己的。

00:44:27.210 --> 00:44:27.670
<v Janice Carter>Yes.
<v Janice Carter>是的。

00:44:27.670 --> 00:44:29.240
<v Robert Krulwich>You lived in a cage?
<v Robert Krulwich>你住在笼子里？

00:44:29.240 --> 00:44:31.380
<v Janice Carter>I lived in a cage. Yes.
<v Janice Carter>我住在笼子里。是的。

00:44:31.380 --> 00:44:41.340
<v Robert Krulwich>Wow.
<v Robert Krulwich>哇。

00:44:41.340 --> 00:44:44.650
<v Jad Abumrad>And in the beginning she says, her cage didn't even have a roof.
<v Jad Abumrad>而且她说，起初她的笼子甚至没有顶。

00:44:44.650 --> 00:44:48.090
<v Janice Carter>No. In the rainy season it rained on me.
<v Janice Carter>不。在雨季，雨直接下在我身上。

00:44:48.090 --> 00:44:52.040
<v Jad Abumrad>The only thing above her head was this fine wire mesh to keep the chimps out.
<v Jad Abumrad>她头顶上唯一的遮挡就是这层细铁丝网，为了把黑猩猩挡在外面。

00:44:52.040 --> 00:45:15.860
<v Janice Carter>The chimps all wanted to be inside with me. When I said, no, then they would climb on top of the cage and sleep out in the open on the wire on top right above me. Every time there was any sound in the night of a hyena or anything, they would immediately squeal and defecate and urinate right on top of me.
<v Janice Carter>黑猩猩都想和我待在里面。我说不，它们就会爬上笼子顶，睡在头顶的铁丝网上。每当晚上有鬣狗或其他什么声音时，它们就会立刻尖叫，并在我头顶上方排泄。

00:45:15.860 --> 00:45:16.440
<v Jad Abumrad>Oh God.
<v Jad Abumrad>噢，上帝。

00:45:16.440 --> 00:45:18.080
<v Robert Krulwich>Really?
<v Robert Krulwich>真的吗？

00:45:18.080 --> 00:45:43.110
<v Janice Carter>Then I put [UNINTELLIGIBLE] on the roof. But then they started dancing on the [UNINTELLIGIBLE]. They really liked the sound that it made. So they were all day long busy dancing. It sounds funny and it was at times. But it distracted them from being chimps.
<v Janice Carter>然后我给屋顶加了[听不清]。但它们开始在那上面跳舞。它们真的很喜欢那种声音。所以它们整天忙着跳舞。这听起来很好笑，有时确实也很好笑。但这让它们分心，没法做一只真正的黑猩猩。

00:45:43.110 --> 00:45:57.280
<v Jad Abumrad>After about a year, says Janice, most of the chimps lost interest in her. Because they couldn't get her tools. She was stuck in a cage. They gave up. They stopped hanging around her and they'd just wonder off into the forest and forage for themselves.
<v Jad Abumrad>大约一年后，珍妮丝说，大多数黑猩猩对她失去了兴趣。因为它们拿不到她的工具。她被关在笼子里。它们放弃了。它们停止围着她转，而是漫步到森林里，自己觅食。

00:45:57.280 --> 00:46:05.740
<v Janice Carter>Yet Lucy did stay behind. Due to the obvious reasons, I think she was different than all the rest of the chimps.
<v Janice Carter>然而露西留下了。因为明显的原因，我觉得她和其他黑猩猩不同。

00:46:05.740 --> 00:46:10.270
<v Jad Abumrad>And so Janice and Lucy entered into a kind of sign language battle of wills.
<v Jad Abumrad>于是珍妮丝和露西展开了一场手语意志力的较量。

00:46:10.270 --> 00:46:22.620
<v Janice Carter>If I came out of the tent to look to see if they were all gone, there she was right there looking really forlorn at me and using sign language to tell me to come out to be with her.
<v Janice Carter>如果我走出帐篷看它们是不是都走了，她就站在那儿，表情忧伤地看着我，并用手语告诉我出来陪她。

00:46:22.620 --> 00:46:25.290
<v Jad Abumrad>But Janice would sign to Lucy, no Lucy, go.
<v Jad Abumrad>但珍妮丝会打手语对露西说，不，露西，走。

00:46:25.290 --> 00:46:26.190
<v Janice Carter>Go.
<v Janice Carter>走。

00:46:26.190 --> 00:46:28.690
<v Jad Abumrad>Lucy would then sign back, no, Janice come.
<v Jad Abumrad>露西会回打手语说，不，珍妮丝，过来。

00:46:28.690 --> 00:46:31.100
<v Janice Carter>No, Lucy go.
<v Janice Carter>不，露西，走。

00:46:31.100 --> 00:46:32.480
<v Jad Abumrad>No Janice come.
<v Jad Abumrad>不，珍妮丝，过来。

00:46:32.480 --> 00:46:33.270
<v Janice Carter>Lucy go.
<v Janice Carter>露西，走。

00:46:33.270 --> 00:46:35.080
<v Jad Abumrad>And this went on and on.
<v Jad Abumrad>这持续了很久。

00:46:35.080 --> 00:46:37.680
<v Janice Carter>I tried and I tried and I tried and I tried.
<v Janice Carter>我试了又试，试了又试。

00:46:37.680 --> 00:46:43.280
<v Jad Abumrad>But Lucy wouldn't move. She would just stand there, waiting for Janice to help her.
<v Jad Abumrad>但露西就是不动。她只是站在那里，等着珍妮丝帮助她。

00:46:43.280 --> 00:47:03.050
<v Janice Carter>Sometimes I would stay inside the tent all day long and I would try to ignore her, ignore that she was there. Thinking that if I ignored her, then she'd go off with the others. But that didn't work. And if I did look at her, then she would sign that she was hurt. She would use the sign for hurt.
<v Janice Carter>有时我会整天待在帐篷里，试图忽略她，忽略她的存在。以为如果我忽略她，她就会和其他伙伴一起离开。但这没用。如果我看着她，她就会用手语说她受伤了。她会用“痛”的手语。

00:47:03.050 --> 00:47:06.480
<v Jad Abumrad>Meanwhile, she wasn't foraging for herself. She was getting thinner.
<v Jad Abumrad>与此同时，她没有自己觅食。她越来越瘦。

00:47:06.480 --> 00:47:54.230
<v Janice Carter>And I tried everything and really, really knocked myself out trying to do things for her. And I just started to think maybe she never was going to do it. And we would argue about it. I ate everything. I was eating ants, I was eating the sticky [? latex ?] and figs. I was doing everyhing that I was trying really nauseating to do just so that she will watch me do it and think, wow, if she's doing it, then I'm going to do it too. And she wouldn't do it. She'd just turn her head away. And I honestly thought at one point that she would rather starve to death than have to work for her food. I was losing hope.
<v Janice Carter>我试了一切，真的耗尽了精力试图为她做点什么。我开始想，也许她永远也不会去做了。我们为此争论。我什么都吃。我吃蚂蚁，我吃那种黏糊糊的[听不清]和无花果。我努力做一切令人作呕的事，只为了让她看着我吃，心想，哇，如果她在吃，那我也要吃。但她就是不吃。她只是转过头去。我真的有一度认为，她宁愿饿死也不愿为了食物而劳动。我当时快绝望了。

00:47:54.230 --> 00:48:05.280
<v Ira Glass>But incredibly, Janice kept at this for years. She'd have to toss Lucy some food, some of her's, just to keep Lucy from starving. But she kept at it.
<v Ira Glass>但令人难以置信的是，珍妮丝坚持了这么多年。她有时会扔给露西一些食物，她自己的一些食物，只是为了让露西不被饿死。但她坚持下来了。

00:48:05.280 --> 00:48:08.980
<v Ira Glass>And then, one evening, after a really, really long day--
<v Ira Glass>然后，在一个傍晚，经过非常非常漫长的一天——

00:48:08.980 --> 00:48:10.720
<v David Segal>Oh, what a drag of a day.
<v David Segal>哦，真是糟糕的一天。

00:48:10.720 --> 00:48:16.470
<v Jad Abumrad>Janice and Lucy are walking through the forest and they both stop because they're so beat. And crash.
<v Jad Abumrad>珍妮丝和露西走在森林里，她们都因为太累而停了下来。晕倒了。

00:48:16.470 --> 00:48:18.660
<v Janice Carter>And we just, we fell asleep.
<v Janice Carter>我们只是，睡着了。

00:48:18.660 --> 00:48:19.390
<v Jad Abumrad>On the ground together.
<v Jad Abumrad>一起躺在地上。

00:48:19.390 --> 00:48:29.010
<v Janice Carter>And when I woke up, Lucy was actually holding my hand. And she had a leaf.
<v Janice Carter>当我醒来时，露西实际上握着我的手。她拿着一片叶子。

00:48:29.010 --> 00:48:30.930
<v Robert Krulwich>She's holding out a leaf?
<v Robert Krulwich>她递出一片叶子？

00:48:30.930 --> 00:48:46.080
<v Janice Carter>Yes. She reached out and she offered it to me. And then I offered it to her. And she ate it. It was a miracle. It was an absolute miracle.
<v Janice Carter>是的。她伸出手递给我。然后我也递给她。她吃了。这是一个奇迹。绝对的奇迹。

00:48:46.080 --> 00:48:47.630
<v Robert Krulwich>And after that says Janice--
<v Robert Krulwich>珍妮丝说，在那之后——

00:48:47.630 --> 00:48:48.770
<v Janice Carter>She was turned.
<v Janice Carter>她彻底变了。

00:48:48.770 --> 00:48:55.810
<v Charles Siebert>And actually, from that moment on Lucy did start to make the effort and go off?
<v Charles Siebert>实际上，从那一刻起，露西确实开始努力去独自生活了？

00:48:55.810 --> 00:48:56.620
<v Jad Abumrad>And be a chimp?
<v Jad Abumrad>去做一只黑猩猩？

00:48:56.620 --> 00:48:57.550
<v Charles Siebert>And be a chimp?
<v Charles Siebert>去做一只黑猩猩？

00:48:57.550 --> 00:48:58.730
<v Jad Abumrad>That's Charles Siebert again.
<v Jad Abumrad>那是查尔斯·西伯特。

00:48:58.730 --> 00:49:03.520
<v Charles Siebert>And it was not too long after that that Janice went away and--
<v Charles Siebert>在那之后没多久，珍妮丝就离开了，并且——

00:49:03.520 --> 00:49:04.340
<v Jad Abumrad>Left the island?
<v Jad Abumrad>离开了那个岛？

00:49:04.340 --> 00:49:11.220
<v Charles Siebert>Yeah.
<v Charles Siebert>是的。

00:49:11.220 --> 00:49:29.220
<v Ira Glass>Janice says she'd periodically circle in a boat just to keep an eye on Lucy. But she says she never, not once, set foot on that island. At least not for a year. Then one day she decided to go back.
<v Ira Glass>珍妮丝说她会定期划船转圈，只是为了照看露西。但她说，她一次也没有，哪怕是一次，踏上那个岛。至少一年内没有。然后有一天她决定回去。

00:49:29.220 --> 00:49:33.170
<v Janice Carter>This day is the first day that I went actually on the island.
<v Janice Carter>这是我实际登上岛的第一天。

00:49:33.170 --> 00:49:46.010
<v Ira Glass>She pulled her boat up to the tip of the island where there's just a little clearing and she parked. And as she did, Lucy and the other chimps who had heard the boat, came out of the forest and into the clearing. And Lucy and her walked toward each other.
<v Ira Glass>她把船划到岛的顶端，那里有一片小空地，她停了下来。当她停下时，听见船声的露西和其他黑猩猩从森林里出来，来到空地上。露西和她朝对方走去。

00:49:46.010 --> 00:49:57.600
<v Janice Carter>And I took with me some of Lucy's possession that had been important to her, like her mirror. And she used to like to draw and books, just to see how she responded to it.
<v Janice Carter>我带了一些露西以前拥有的、对她很重要的东西，比如她的镜子。她以前喜欢画画，还有书，只是想看看她会有什么反应。

00:49:57.600 --> 00:49:59.000
<v Ira Glass>And what did she do?
<v Ira Glass>她做了什么？

00:49:59.000 --> 00:50:49.930
<v Janice Carter>Well she looked at the things. She looked at the book. She looked at herself in the mirror and she signed to herself in the mirror. Then all of a sudden she grabbed me. I mean really grabbed me. One arm circled all the way around me and she sort of held me really, really tight. It really made me breathless and I started crying. She started begin these soft little pants. And I'm still pretty certain what she was saying to me was it's OK. You know, it's all OK now.
<v Janice Carter>嗯，她看着那些东西。她看书。她照镜子看着自己，然后对着镜子里的自己打手语。然后突然她抓住了我。我是说真的抓住了我。一只手臂完全环绕着我，她紧紧地抱着我。这让我喘不过气来，我开始哭泣。她开始发出那种柔和的喘息声。我相信她当时对我说的肯定是“没关系”。你知道，现在一切都好起来了。

00:50:49.930 --> 00:51:04.450
<v Jad Abumrad>At that moment, somebody in Janice's boat snapped a picture of her and Lucy hugging. It's a picture that Charles Siebert printed in his book. And it's one of those images that when you see it, I don't know why, it just haunts you.
<v Jad Abumrad>就在那一刻，珍妮丝船上的某个人拍下了她和露西拥抱的照片。那是查尔斯·西伯特在他书中印出的照片。那是一张当你看到时，我不知道为什么，它就是会萦绕在你心头的照片。

00:51:04.450 --> 00:51:08.470
<v Jad Abumrad>Lucy has their head against Janice's chest and Janice has her arms around Lucy.
<v Jad Abumrad>露西把头靠在珍妮丝的胸前，珍妮丝双臂环抱着露西。

00:51:08.470 --> 00:51:23.150
<v Charles Siebert>It's one of the more fraught moments. You have to just look at the picture. I mean it sort of made me want to write the book. Something about the complexity and the invertedness of that picture.
<v Charles Siebert>这是最令人揪心的时刻之一。你必须看看那张照片。它让我有了写这本书的冲动。照片中那种复杂和颠倒的关系。

00:51:23.150 --> 00:51:44.250
<v Janice Carter>After that, the other chimps had started to go. And she wanted to go with them. And she got up and she didn't turn back to look at me. She just kept walking. She wanted to go with the other chimps and she did.
<v Janice Carter>在那之后，其他黑猩猩开始出发了。她想和它们一起走。她站起来，没有回头看我。她只是继续走。她想去追随其他黑猩猩，她做到了。

00:51:44.250 --> 00:51:52.480
<v Jad Abumrad>A year later, Janice went back to visit Lucy again. But when she got there, this time Lucy was gone.
<v Jad Abumrad>一年后，珍妮丝又去探望露西。但当她到那里时，这次露西不见了。

00:51:52.480 --> 00:51:59.530
<v Janice Carter>And I went to all the different places looking to see if we could find anything. And we did. We found her body.
<v Janice Carter>我去了所有不同的地方寻找，看看能不能找到什么线索。我们确实找到了。我们找到了她的遗骸。

00:51:59.530 --> 00:52:06.850
<v Jad Abumrad>She was lying right near the place where Janice's cage had been, just a skeleton.
<v Jad Abumrad>她躺在珍妮丝曾经住过笼子的地方附近，只剩下一具骨架。

00:52:06.850 --> 00:52:15.580
<v Janice Carter>Her skull and her hands and her feet were separated from the rest of her skeleton.
<v Janice Carter>她的头骨、手和脚与骨架的其他部分分开了。

00:52:15.580 --> 00:52:18.990
<v Robert Krulwich>So how did you know that that was her body?
<v Robert Krulwich>那么你怎么知道那就是她的遗体？

00:52:18.990 --> 00:52:26.750
<v David Segal>She had a split between her front teeth and she was very long. And there was nobody else missing.
<v David Segal>她的前牙之间有缝隙，而且她长得很长。没有其他人失踪了。

00:52:26.750 --> 00:52:29.930
<v Jad Abumrad>And maybe the saddest, strangest thing was that--
<v Jad Abumrad>也许最令人伤心、最奇怪的是——

00:52:29.930 --> 00:52:34.690
<v Janice Carter>We didn't find any signs of her skin or hair.
<v Janice Carter>我们没有发现任何皮肤或毛发的迹象。

00:52:34.690 --> 00:52:37.960
<v Jad Abumrad>It appeared that Lucy had been skinned.
<v Jad Abumrad>看起来露西被剥皮了。

00:52:37.960 --> 00:53:07.910
<v Charles Siebert>And no one knows actually what happened. But because the hands were taken, which poachers do, they thought one of the conjectures which makes it really unbelievably tragic is that they think that Lucy, always the first to approach humans, just sort of guilelessly approached poachers, not knowing that they were that. And that they just took advantage of their unwitting and over eager prey. But that was Lucy's end.
<v Charles Siebert>没有人知道到底发生了什么。但因为她的手被拿走了，这是偷猎者会做的，他们推测，其中一个推测让这件事变得令人难以置信地悲惨，他们认为露西，总是第一个接近人类，只是单纯地接近了偷猎者，不知道他们是谁。他们只是利用了她这种毫无防备且过于热情的猎物。这就是露西的结局。

00:53:07.910 --> 00:54:00.600
<v Janice Carter>The scenario that I have developed to cope with her death is that a fisherman or someone, some local person that just happened to pull up next to the land and was going to take a break or [UNINTELLIGIBLE PHRASE] or do something. And because she always felt confidence around humans, she probably approached the person. Perhaps she surprised the person and just on reflexes, defense, she was probably shot. I've got no other explanation.
<v Janice Carter>我为应对她的死亡而构建的一个假设是，一个渔民或某个人，某个当地人，正好把船停在岸边打算休息一下，或者做些什么。因为她总是对人类感到自信，她可能接近了那个人。也许她吓到了那个人，出于反射防御，她可能被射杀了。我没有其他解释。

00:54:00.600 --> 00:54:16.280
<v Ira Glass>Janice Carter still lives in Gambia. It's then when she realized that the chimps there would never have a chance to survive unless their human neighbors understood that might be a good thing. So these days she spends a lot of her time teaching people in Gambia about chimps, to convince them about the importance of protecting the chimps habitat.
<v Ira Glass>珍妮丝·卡特依然住在冈比亚。那是她意识到，除非当地人类邻居明白保护黑猩猩是件好事，否则那里的黑猩猩永远没有生存的机会。所以这些日子，她花了很多时间在冈比亚教育人们关于黑猩猩的知识，让他们相信保护黑猩猩栖息地的重要性。

00:54:16.280 --> 00:54:23.460
<v Ira Glass>Charles Siebert's latest book, all about the midway point between animals and humans is The Wauchula Woods Accord.
<v Ira Glass>查尔斯·西伯特关于人类与动物之间中间地带的最新著作是《瓦丘拉森林协定》。

00:54:23.460 --> 00:54:38.550
